Careful with char boundaries — all ASCII here mostly; but rule says never byte-index. I'll implement with `rsplit_once('-')` and check the suffix is all digits and len 8, then return prefix. For `-2025-05-14`, rsplit_once gives ("...-2025-05","14"). Hmm. Handle: if suffix is 2 digits and next is `-MM-DD`... Let me keep it to: strip trailing `-YYYYMMDD`; and strip trailing `-YYYY-MM-DD` by using strip_suffix with an 11-char check. I'll write:

```rust
fn strip_release_suffix(id: &str) -> &str {
    // -YYYYMMDD
    if let Some((head, tail)) = id.rsplit_once('-') {
        if tail.len() == 8 && tail.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()) {
            return head;
        }
        // -YYYY-MM-DD : tail is DD, and head ends with -YYYY-MM
        if tail.len() == 2 && tail.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()) {
            if let Some((h2, t2)) = head.rsplit_once('-') {
                if t2.len() == 2 && t2.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()) {
                    if let Some((h3, t3)) = h2.rsplit_once('-') {
                        if t3.len() == 4 && t3.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()) {
                            return h3;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    id
}
```

That's safe (slicing at '-' boundaries is fine, and `head` is a str slice).

